<article class="env-lemma" id="0BI0">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BI0">Lemma <span data-tag="0BI0">10.37.9</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a Noetherian normal domain. Then $R[[x]]$ is a Noetherian normal domain. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The power series ring is Noetherian by Lemma <a href="/tag/0306" data-tag="0306">10.31.2</a>. Let $f, g \in R[[x]]$ be nonzero elements such that $w = f/g$ is integral over $R[[x]]$. Let $K$ be the fraction field of $R$. Since the ring of Laurent series $K((x)) = K[[x]][1/x]$ is a field, we can write $w = a_ n x^ n + a_{n + 1} x^{n + 1} + \ldots $ for some $n \in \mathbf{Z}$, $a_ i \in K$, and $a_ n \not= 0$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/00GX" data-tag="00GX">10.37.4</a> we see there exists a nonzero element $h = b_ m x^ m + b_{m + 1} x^{m + 1} + \ldots $ in $R[[x]]$ with $b_ m \not= 0$ such that $w^ e h \in R[[x]]$ for all $e \geq 1$. We conclude that $n \geq 0$ and that $b_ m a_ n^ e \in R$ for all $e \geq 1$. Since $R$ is Noetherian this implies that $a_ n \in R$ by the same lemma. Now, if $a_ n, a_{n + 1}, \ldots , a_{N - 1} \in R$, then we can apply the same argument to $w - a_ n x^ n - \ldots - a_{N - 1} x^{N - 1} = a_ N x^ N + \ldots $. In this way we see that all $a_ i \in R$ and the lemma is proved. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>